[{"kind":1,"language":"markdown","value":"# Setup\n\nThe RESPOND script is a large SAS script used to generate datasets corresponding to the Massachusetts population at high risk for opioid use disorder (OUD). The setup alone incorporates various codes, several setup macros, filtering unknowns, and data privacy suppression.\n\n## Script Logic\n\nOverall, the logic behind the known capture is fairly simple: search through individual databases and flag if an ICD9, ICD10, CPT, NDC, or other specialized code matches our lookup table. If a record has one of these codes, it is 'flagged' for OUD. The utilized databases are then joined onto the SPINE demographics dataset and if the sum of flags is greater than zero, then the record is flagged with OUD. At current iteration, data being pulled through this method is stratified by Year (or Year and Month), Race, Sex, and Age.","outputs":[]},{"kind":1,"language":"markdown","value":"## Global Variables\n\nOur script starts off by assigning global variables. These are intended to allow a user to control the data being generated without having to change sections throughout the code. Notably, we have the variables `MOUD_leniency` and `DOC_leniency`. These control the point at which we consider \"new\" MOUD or corrections engagement. When set to 30, `MOUD_leniency` would assume there is a 30 day gap between when someone last was identified as engaged with MOUD and when they should be considered \"off\" MOUD.","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"language":"sas","value":"/*==============================*/\n/*  \tGLOBAL VARIABLES   \t\t*/\n/*==============================*/\n%LET year=(2015:2023);\n%LET MOUD_leniency=30;\n%LET DOC_leniency=35;\n%LET today=%sysfunc(today(), date9.);\n%LET formatted_date=%sysfunc(translate(&today, %str(_), %str(/)));\n%LET respond_outdir=/sas/data/DPH/OPH/PHD/FOLDERS/SUBSTANCE_USE_CODE/RESPOND/RESPOND UPDATE;","outputs":[]},{"kind":1,"language":"markdown","value":"## Macros\n\nSAS differs from many modern computing languages, specifically when it comes to reproducible code. The solution in SAS is referred to as macros. These operate as a preprocessor find/replace and can take variables. We define 3 macros: \n\n1. `dedup_tables`\n2. `finalize_oud_input`\n3. `export_csv`\n\nThe first one, `dedup_tables` focuses on removing duplicate rows from the dataset and creating a table with the resulting data. The second, `finalize_oud_input`, focuses on generating the counts from the OUD population. The third, `export_csv`, writes the data to the specified file.","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"language":"sas","value":"/*==============================*/\n/*      UTILITY MACROS          */\n/*==============================*/\n%MACRO dedup_tables(list=);\n    PROC SQL;\n        %LOCAL i ds;\n        %DO i=1 %TO %SYSFUNC(countw(&list, %STR( )));\n            %LET ds=%SCAN(&list, &i, %STR( ));\n            CREATE TABLE &ds AS SELECT DISTINCT * FROM &ds;\n        %END;\n    QUIT;\n%MEND;\n\n%MACRO finalize_oud_input(in_ds=, out_ds=);\n    DATA &out_ds;\n        SET &in_ds;\n\n        ARRAY oud_flags {*} oud_apcd oud_cm oud_death oud_matris oud_pmp oud_bsas\n            oud_pharm;\n\n        DO i=1 TO dim(oud_flags);\n            IF oud_flags[i]=9999 THEN oud_flags[i]=0;\n        END;\n\n        oud_cnt=sum(oud_apcd, oud_cm, oud_death, oud_matris, oud_pmp, oud_bsas,\n            oud_pharm);\n        IF oud_cnt > 0 THEN oud_master=1;\n        ELSE oud_master=0;\n        IF oud_master=0 THEN DELETE;\n\n        age=year - YOB;\n        age_grp_five=put(age, age_grps_five.);\n        age_grp_twenty=put(age, age_grps_twenty.);\n    RUN;\n%MEND;\n\n%MACRO export_csv(data=, name=);\n    PROC EXPORT DATA=&data OUTFILE=\"&respond_outdir/&name._&formatted_date..csv\"\n        DBMS=csv REPLACE;\n    RUN;\n%MEND;","outputs":[]},{"kind":1,"language":"markdown","value":"## Codings\n\nThe Massachusetts Department of Public Health retains a lot of data in various encodings. This section attempts to explain how the RESPOND script expects these bits of data.\n\n### ICD Diagnostic and Procedure Codes\n\nArguably, the most well known encodings we worry about with PHD are the ICD codes. ICD (International Classification of Diseases) codes have various revisions. We make use of the 9th and 10th revisions (ICD-9 and ICD-10 respectively). They are issued by the [Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services](https://www.cms.gov/medicare/coordination-benefits-recovery/overview/icd-code-lists).\n\n> The ICD-9 and ICD-10 valid and excluded diagnosis codes for the latest fiscal year are made available to non-group health plan (NGHP) responsible reporting entities (RREs) and agents for Section 111 liability insurance (including self-insurance), no-fault, and workers’ compensation mandatory reporting.\n\nWe utilize ICD Codes to isolate opioid use disorder. The lists below were provided by DPH as the \"definition of Opioid Use Disorder (OUD)\" on April 1, 2026.\n\n#### Diagnostic Codes\n\n> \"30400\", \"30401\", \"30402\", \n> \"30470\", \"30471\", \"30472\", \n> \"30550\", \"30551\", \"30552\", \n> \"F1110\", \"F11120\", \"F11121\", \"F11122\", \"F11129\", \"F1113\", \"F1114\", \"F11150\", \"F11151\", \n> \"F11159\", \"F11181\", \"F11182\", \"F11188\", \"F1119\", \"F1120\", \"F11220\", \"F11221\", \"F11222\",  \n> \"F11229\", \"F1123\", \"F1124\", \"F11250\", \"F11251\", \"F11259\", \"F11281\", \"F11282\", \"F11288\", \"F1129\" \n> \"E8500\", \"E8501\", \"E8502\", \"96500\", \"96501\", \"96502\", \"96509\", \"9701\",  \n> \"T400X1A\", \"T400X2A\", \"T400X3A\", \"T400X4A\", \n> \"T400X1D\", \"T400X2D\", \"T400X3D\", \"T400X4D\", \n> \"T401X1A\", \"T401X2A\", \"T401X3A\", \"T401X4A\", \n> \"T401X1D\", \"T401X2D\", \"T401X3D\", \"T401X4D\", \n> \"T402X1A\", \"T402X2A\", \"T402X3A\", \"T402X4A\", \n> \"T402X1D\", \"T402X2D\", \"T402X3D\", \"T402X4D\", \n> \"T403X1A\", \"T403X2A\", \"T403X3A\", \"T403X4A\", \n> \"T403X1D\", \"T403X2D\", \"T403X3D\", \"T403X4D\", \n> \"T404X1A\", \"T404X2A\", \"T404X3A\", \"T404X4A\", \n> \"T404X1D\", \"T404X2D\", \"T404X3D\", \"T404X4D\", \n> \"T40601A\", \"T40601D\", \"T40602A\", \"T40602D\", \n> \"T40603A\", \"T40603D\", \"T40604A\", \"T40604D\", \n> \"T40691A\", \"T40692A\", \"T40693A\", \"T40694A\", \n> \"T40691D\", \"T40692D\", \"T40693D\", \"T40694D\",\n\n#### Procedure Codes \n\n> \"G2215\", \"G2216\", \"G1028\", \"J0592\", \n> \"G2068\", \"G2069\", \"G2070\", \"G2071\", \"G2072\", \"G2079\", \n> \"J0570\", \"J0571\", \"J0572\", \"J0573\", \"J0574\", \"J0575\", \n> \"Q9991\", \"Q9992\", \"H0020\", \"G2067\", \"G2078\", \"S0109\", \n> \"HZ91ZZZ\", \"HZ81ZZZ\" \n\n### NDC Codes\n\nFollowing ICD codes, we have NDC (National Drug Code) codes. These codes are assigned to all human drugs in the US and issued by the [US Food and Drug Administration](https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-approvals-and-databases/national-drug-code-directory). We care about NDCs particularly for prescriptions issued in the prescription monitoring program dataset (PMP). Notably, we pay attention to buprenorphine administered as an MOUD (e.g. `BUP_CAT_PMP=1`).","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"language":"sas","value":"/*===========AGE================*/\nPROC FORMAT;\n    VALUE age_grps_five low-5='1' 6-10='2' 11-15='3' 16-20='4' 21-25='5' 26-30=\n        '6' 31-35='7' 36-40='8' 41-45='9' 46-50='10' 51-55='11' 56-60='12' 61-65\n        ='13' 66-70='14' 71-75='15' 76-80='16' 81-85='17' 86-90='18' 91-95='19'\n        96-998='20' 999='999';\n\nPROC FORMAT;\n    VALUE age_grps_twenty low-20='1' 21-40='2' 41-60='3' 61-80='4' 81-998='5'\n        999='999';\n\n    /*========ICD CODES=============*/\n    /* Removed Codes from ICD list per PHD definition of OUD 4/2/26:\n    '30403', '30473', '30553', 'F1111', 'F1121', 'F1193', 'F1199'\n\n    Overdose Codes:\n    'T40411A', 'T40411D', 'T40412A', 'T40412D',\n    'T40413A', 'T40413D', 'T40414A', 'T40414D',\n    'T40421A', 'T40421D', 'T40422A', 'T40422D',\n    'T40423A', 'T40423D', 'T40424A', 'T40424D',\n    'T40491A', 'T40491D', 'T40492A', 'T40492D',\n    'T40493A', 'T40493D', 'T40494A', 'T40494D'\n\n    Additional RESPOND:\n    'F1193','F1199'\n\n    Removed Codes from Procedure list per PHD definition of OUD 4/2/26:\n    'G2073','G2074','G2075','G2076','G2077', 'G2080','G2081'\n\n     */\n    %LET ICD=('30400','30401','30402',\n        '30470','30471','30472','30550','30551','30552',\n        'F1110','F11120','F11121','F11122','F11129','F1113','F1114','F11150','F11151','F11159','F11181','F11182','F11188','F1119','F1120','F11220','F11221','F11222',\n        'F11229','F1123','F1124','F11250','F11251','F11259','F11281','F11282',\n        'F11288','F1129', 'E8500','E8501','E8502','96500','96501','96502',\n        '96509','9701', 'T400X1A','T400X2A','T400X3A','T400X4A',\n        'T400X1D','T400X2D','T400X3D','T400X4D',\n        'T401X1A','T401X2A','T401X3A','T401X4A',\n        'T401X1D','T401X2D','T401X3D','T401X4D',\n        'T402X1A','T402X2A','T402X3A','T402X4A',\n        'T402X1D','T402X2D','T402X3D','T402X4D',\n        'T403X1A','T403X2A','T403X3A','T403X4A',\n        'T403X1D','T403X2D','T403X3D','T403X4D',\n        'T404X1A','T404X2A','T404X3A','T404X4A',\n        'T404X1D','T404X2D','T404X3D','T404X4D',\n        'T40601A','T40601D','T40602A','T40602D',\n        'T40603A','T40603D','T40604A','T40604D',\n        'T40691A','T40692A','T40693A','T40694A',\n        'T40691D','T40692D','T40693D','T40694D');\n\n    %LET PROC=('G2215','G2216','G1028','J0592', 'G2068','G2069','G2070',\n        'G2071','G2072','G2079','J0570','J0571','J0572','J0573','J0574','J0575','Q9991','Q9992','H0020','G2067','G2078','S0109','HZ81ZZZ','HZ91ZZZ');\n\n    %LET bsas_drugs=(5,6,7,21,22,23,24,26);\n\n/*\nTake NDC codes where buprenorphine has been identified,\ninsert them into BUP_NDC as a macro variable\n */\nPROC SQL;\n    CREATE TABLE ndc AS SELECT DISTINCT NDC FROM PHDPMP.PMP WHERE BUP_CAT_PMP=1;\nQUIT;\n\nPROC SQL noprint;\n    SELECT quote(trim(ndc), \"'\") into :BUP_NDC separated by ',' FROM ndc;\nQUIT;\n","outputs":[]},{"kind":1,"language":"markdown","value":"## Demographics\n\nAs RESPOND has heavy interest in studying OUD impacts on various subgroups, we often focus on various demographic encodings. In our case, we put particular emphasis on age, race/ethnicity, and sex encodings.\n\n### Age Groups\n\nOften times, age is extracted from individual reports via the SPINE database in the PHD. As single age variables often lead to suppression, we refer to age groups. Traditionally, we focus on five year age groups (e.g. 1-5, 6-10, 11-15, etc.) and twenty year age groups (e.g. 1-20, 21-40, 41-60, etc.). These are treated as contiguous arrays and index accordingly. In other words, to get the 11-15 year old population from the five year age groups, we refer to them as group 3. To access the 21-40 year olds from the twenty year groups we refer to group 2.\n\n### PHD SPINE\n\nThe SPINE is a dataset in PHD that maintains demographic connections. Thus, if we want to extract demographic data, we must interact with the SPINE. From the SPINE, we pull the IDs, Race/Ethnicity (re) encodings, sex encodings, and year-of-birth (YOB) values. We have filters in the case that sex or race/ethnicity is 9 or 99 (unknown, NA, prefer not to say, etc.).\n\nAfter extracting the encodings, a shell table is built so that it can be filled in via the data pulls.","outputs":[]},{"kind":2,"language":"sas","value":"PROC FORMAT;\n    VALUE age_grps_five low-5='1' 6-10='2' 11-15='3' 16-20='4' 21-25='5' 26-30=\n        '6' 31-35='7' 36-40='8' 41-45='9' 46-50='10' 51-55='11' 56-60='12' 61-65\n        ='13' 66-70='14' 71-75='15' 76-80='16' 81-85='17' 86-90='18' 91-95='19'\n        96-998='20' 999='999';\n\nPROC FORMAT;\n    VALUE age_grps_twenty low-20='1' 21-40='2' 41-60='3' 61-80='4' 81-998='5'\n        999='999';\n/*\nUsing data from PHDSPINE.DEMO, take the cartesian coordinate of years\n(as defined above) and months 1:12 to construct a shell table\n */\nDATA demographics;\n    SET PHDSPINE.DEMO (KEEP=ID FINAL_RE FINAL_SEX YOB);\n    IF FINAL_RE=9 THEN DELETE;\n    IF FINAL_RE=99 THEN DELETE;\n\n    IF FINAL_SEX=9 THEN DELETE;\n    IF FINAL_SEX=99 THEN DELETE;\nRUN;\n\n%let start_year=%scan(%substr(&year,2,%length(&year)-2),1,':');\n%let end_year=%scan(%substr(&year,2,%length(&year)-2),2,':');\n\nDATA months;\n    DO month=1 to 12;\n        OUTPUT;\n    END;\nRUN;\n\nDATA years;\n    DO year=&start_year to &end_year;\n        OUTPUT;\n    END;\nRUN;\n\nPROC SQL;\n    CREATE TABLE demographics_monthly AS SELECT * FROM demographics, months,\n        years;\nQUIT;\n\nPROC SQL;\n    CREATE TABLE demographics_yearly AS SELECT * FROM demographics, years;\nQUIT;","outputs":[]}]